WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.549
Picture driving home late on a quiet highway.

2
00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:07.385
Three lights come on at once.

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.241
Oil pressure low.

4
00:00:09.241 --> 00:00:10.881
Washer fluid low.

5
00:00:10.881 --> 00:00:13.487
A service is due in 500 km.

6
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.379
Only one of them means pull over now, and you have about two seconds to know which.

7
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.403
Software teams face that moment every night.

8
00:00:21.403 --> 00:00:24.715
So which problems should wake an engineer at 3 a.m.?

9
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:26.544
Four candidates.

10
00:00:26.544 --> 00:00:28.280
CPU above ninety percent.

11
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.225
A disk seventy percent full.

12
00:00:30.225 --> 00:00:32.586
Five percent of checkouts failing.

13
00:00:32.586 --> 00:00:36.752
And one pod, a running copy of the app, that restarted once.

14
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:40.886
Hold that guess.

15
00:00:40.886 --> 00:00:45.661
To answer it properly, we first need a number that says what working means.

16
00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:49.012
It is easy to treat every light as urgent.

17
00:00:49.012 --> 00:00:55.561
Then, after the tenth false alarm, you ignore them all, like a driver who tapes over the warning lights.

18
00:00:55.561 --> 00:00:56.947
That is alert fatigue.

19
00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.435
Start with what users feel.

20
00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:08.539
For hello-devops, our tiny lab app, count the good answers at slash, the ones that are not a server failure, and divide by all the answers.

21
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:12.933
That ratio is called an SLI, a service level indicator.

22
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:16.895
A not found, for a mistyped address, is the user's mistake.

23
00:01:16.895 --> 00:01:18.791
It does not count against you.

24
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.369
Now give it a target and a window.

25
00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:24.783
Ninety nine point nine percent, over thirty days.

26
00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:28.684
A target like that is an SLO, a service level objective.

27
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.094
Why not a hundred?

28
00:01:30.094 --> 00:01:35.204
Users rarely notice, because their own phone and network fail far more often.

29
00:01:35.204 --> 00:01:37.859
And each extra nine costs far more work.

30
00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:40.513
Here is a picture that helps.

31
00:01:40.513 --> 00:01:44.141
Think of a phone plan with ten gigabytes of data a month.

32
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:47.040
You do not panic about every video you watch.

33
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:50.476
You panic when half the data is gone on day three.

34
00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:52.852
It is not the amount that scares you.

35
00:01:52.852 --> 00:01:53.879
It is the speed.

36
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:54.573
Map it.

37
00:01:54.573 --> 00:01:59.374
The data allowance is the failure your target allows: the error budget.

38
00:01:59.374 --> 00:02:01.741
The month is the thirty day window.

39
00:02:01.741 --> 00:02:04.175
How fast it drains is the burn rate.

40
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.995
So how big is the budget?

41
00:02:06.995 --> 00:02:09.581
A hundred percent, minus the target.

42
00:02:09.581 --> 00:02:10.873
Point one percent.

43
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.179
Say two hundred thousand requests a day, my guess for the example.

44
00:02:15.179 --> 00:02:19.135
Thirty days hold six million, and six thousand of them may fail.

45
00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:23.824
If everything fails at once, that is point one percent of a month's minutes.

46
00:02:23.824 --> 00:02:26.463
Forty three point two minutes of full outage.

47
00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:30.454
One bad deploy breaks everything for twenty minutes.

48
00:02:30.454 --> 00:02:32.293
How much of the month is gone?

49
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.230
Almost half.

50
00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:41.280
Twenty out of forty three point two is forty six percent, in one evening.

51
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.304
So the point is, a rollback has to take minutes, not an hour.

52
00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:47.014
Now the speed.

53
00:02:47.014 --> 00:02:51.636
The burn rate is the error ratio right now, divided by the budget.

54
00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:55.265
Here is a month, and the budget left, from full to empty.

55
00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:58.402
Point one percent failing is burn rate one.

56
00:02:58.402 --> 00:03:00.927
The budget lasts exactly thirty days.

57
00:03:00.927 --> 00:03:02.633
That is the plan working.

58
00:03:02.867 --> 00:03:05.810
Point six percent failing is burn rate six.

59
00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:08.000
The budget is gone in five days.

60
00:03:08.233 --> 00:03:12.545
And one point four four percent is burn rate fourteen point four.

61
00:03:12.545 --> 00:03:14.071
Gone in about two days.

62
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.151
Same plan.

63
00:03:15.151 --> 00:03:17.108
Only the speed changed.

64
00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:20.733
You might be thinking: why the fancy ratio?

65
00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:23.999
Why not just alert when more than ten requests fail?

66
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.794
Because ten failures mean nothing on a busy shop, and a lot on a quiet one.

67
00:03:28.794 --> 00:03:32.469
Burn rate is already scaled to your traffic and your target.

68
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.829
And why fourteen point four?

69
00:03:34.829 --> 00:03:37.484
A month has seven hundred and twenty hours.

70
00:03:37.484 --> 00:03:41.931
At that speed, one single hour spends two percent of the month's budget.

71
00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:44.906
Now we can sort the lights.

72
00:03:44.906 --> 00:03:47.751
Every alert goes into one of three boxes.

73
00:03:47.751 --> 00:03:49.624
Page, ticket, or dashboard.

74
00:03:49.833 --> 00:03:58.352
Google's SRE Workbook suggests: page someone at burn rate fourteen point four over one hour, or six over six hours.

75
00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:03.672
Open a ticket, a task for working hours, at burn rate one over three days.

76
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:04.533
Try one.

77
00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:07.775
Two percent of requests fail for an hour.

78
00:04:07.775 --> 00:04:09.040
Page, or ticket?

79
00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:15.719
Two percent over point one percent is burn rate twenty.

80
00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:18.239
Above fourteen point four, so it pages.

81
00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:20.341
Back to our four lights.

82
00:04:20.341 --> 00:04:23.819
Failed checkouts are something users feel, right now.

83
00:04:23.819 --> 00:04:24.803
That is a page.

84
00:04:25.033 --> 00:04:29.180
The other three are possible causes, which may never hurt anyone.

85
00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:33.836
A busy server that answers every request quickly is simply doing its job.

86
00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:37.359
So they go on a screen you look at, or on a to-do list.

87
00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:39.655
Wake people for what users feel.

88
00:04:39.655 --> 00:04:41.612
Put the causes on a dashboard.

89
00:04:42.833 --> 00:04:43.986
A few guardrails.

90
00:04:43.986 --> 00:04:48.190
A page must be urgent, real, and something a human can act on.

91
00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:51.920
If nobody needs to act, delete it, or make it a ticket.

92
00:04:52.133 --> 00:04:55.077
When the budget is spent, pause risky releases.

93
00:04:55.077 --> 00:04:58.397
And a budget you never touch is speed you never used.

94
00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.751
So the moral of the story.

95
00:05:00.751 --> 00:05:06.085
Count good answers, set a target, and treat the failure it allows as your data plan.

96
00:05:06.085 --> 00:05:10.085
What wakes people is not a failure, but the speed of the drain.

97
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:11.701
A quick check.

98
00:05:11.701 --> 00:05:14.726
Point three percent of requests fail, all week.

99
00:05:14.726 --> 00:05:18.073
What is the burn rate, and is it a page or a ticket?

100
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.352
Burn rate three.

101
00:05:22.352 --> 00:05:24.224
The budget lasts ten days.

102
00:05:24.224 --> 00:05:28.472
Nobody needs to wake up, but someone must fix it this week.

103
00:05:28.472 --> 00:05:29.120
A ticket.
